docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:1:| # | Severity P1/P2/P3 | Section | Defect | Concrete fix |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:3:| 1 | P1 | §2.4 D1 | **D1 bypasses existing publication conditions.** The flag permits the snapshot API to expose stored results, but P1 §3.2 explicitly requires F4 cards, forbids percentages without `display.percent`, and forbids fallback to macro numbers when F4 fails. F4 §4.1 makes `publishable` a licensing gate for **all judgment cards**, not merely probabilities. Showing scores while `publishable=false` contradicts these rules and the owner’s “只有满足既有发布条件才展示”. | Require the server flag **and** a publishable, eligible F4 judgment bound to the same snapshot/method before displaying judgment numbers. Explicitly revise P1’s percentage policy if non-probability score shares are desired; do not reinterpret the existing gate. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:4:| 2 | P1 | §§3.2–3.3 | The client-importable view calls `matchRule`, reconstructs rule inputs, computes coverage and normalisation, and decides whether classification should have matched. This introduces client-side judgment computation despite §3.7. Reusing the matcher avoids a duplicate rule table, but does not satisfy the stated boundary. | Produce the decomposition/status projection server-side using version-selected inputs. Keep client work to formatting, sorting and navigation. Perform reconciliation on the server; never substitute its computed values for stored results. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:5:| 3 | P1 | §§3.3–3.4 | `regionWeights(region)` reads **current code**, not snapshot-time weights. The proposed `{version, weights}` cannot truthfully claim historical provenance. Current catalog status/name/unit also misdescribes old snapshots after WP-D changes missing rows to active. A rules-version check does not catch catalog or methodology mismatch. | Read the catalog frozen under `snapshot.catalog_version` from `macro_catalog_versions`, select the matching methodology weights, and return frozen metadata. Reject unsupported versions explicitly. Iterate the complete historical weight set, including missing/restricted rows. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:9:| 7 | P1 | §§3.3, 3.8, 8 | Reconciliation checks only contribution sums and normalisation. It can pass with wrong support coefficients, duplicate rows, an observation belonging to another series, wrong coverage/uncovered weight, or inconsistent primary/secondary/margin. Nevertheless §8 claims complete arithmetic and snapshot proof. | Validate unique weighted rows, manifest membership and series identity, `weight × support = contribution`, complete denominator, matched plus uncovered weight, stored coverage, ranking and margin using defined rounding tolerances. Add deliberate corruption cases for each invariant. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:13:| 11 | P1 | §§3.3, 5.7, 10 | Formula-hash drift begins in **WP-B**, not only WP-D: `macroRegimeV1.deps` explicitly includes `snapshots.ts`, which WP-B refactors. WP-D also changes other dependencies. Unchanged numerical output does not prevent `methodology-sync` refusing the changed hash. | Include a method-version/hash migration strategy for every PR touching the closure or declared dependencies. Test sync against the previously registered row, not merely fresh registration. Do not describe P5-B as independently safe without this dependency. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:14:| 12 | P1 | §5.7 | Importing v1’s implementation functions and changing v2’s params will not work. Those functions are not exported, and both `gather` and `compute` close over **`macroRegimeV1.params.catalog_version`**. Reusing its object methods preserves the old pin too. | Implement a version-bound factory or independent v2 functions with an immutable explicit catalog pin. Test v2 through gather → compute → registration, including rejection of v1 snapshots. Account for the resulting v1 hash impact. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:15:| 13 | P1 | §§5.7, 8 | Removing v1 from `REGISTRY` avoids one sync comparison but does not preserve historical execution. v1 imports the mutable classifier/catalog, and `classifyAsOf` uses current catalog inputs. The spec changes v1 fixtures while claiming historical replay is unchanged. | Preserve original v1 fixtures and executable version bindings. Add v2 fixtures separately, define retirement explicitly, and prove that an existing v1 judgment can still replay/settle after the catalog upgrade—or state a concrete supported migration limitation. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:16:| 14 | P1 | §4.4 | Preset responses contain `inputs_hash` but neither frozen inputs nor a durable manifest reference. `CorrelationResult` currently requires `inputs`. `snapshotId` is insufficient: POST reads full histories at a new wall-clock cutoff, including assets/events outside the overview manifest. The displayed result cannot be replayed from its response. | Return a complete frozen input receipt or a durable immutable manifest reference, including cutoff and catalog/code/method versions. Bind computation to that receipt rather than merely labelling it with the active snapshot ID. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:18:| 16 | P2 | §§4.4, 4.7 | “No stored runs” satisfies only half of P1 §3.4: P1 also says **不自动重算**. This route automatically computes results and bypasses run accounting for the eight presets. It is not an arbitrary-analysis quota bypass if strictly allowlisted, but process-local caching does not bound cold-start/multi-instance DB and compute cost. | Explicitly amend the product/quota policy for shared preset results. Specify authentication, a fixed preset allowlist, read/code-version/error limits, shared caching or request limits, and cost measurements. Define an actual per-preset API before promising “逐张加载”. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:20:| 18 | P1 | §5.2 | WTREGEN is labelled `usd_bn`, with no conversion, but the primary series is **millions of U.S. dollars**, weekly ending Wednesday. This produces a 1,000× unit error and can quarantine valid values against the proposed 10,000 maximum. [FRED WTREGEN](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/WTREGEN) | Use `usd_mn` for native and derived WTREGEN, or introduce an explicit versioned ÷1,000 derivation with provenance. Test a known source value and its rendered unit. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:22:| 20 | P1 | §§5.3–5.5 | The migration plan targets a nonexistent `macro_series.adapter` CHECK. The actual adapter restriction is on `macro_evidence_objects.adapter`. It also omits the `macro_series.derive` CHECK for `effective_interval`, and changing the locator column CHECK alone leaves `macro_write_observations`’ locator allowlist rejecting `html_text`. | Enumerate and migrate the real table constraints and RPC validations. Include end-to-end database tests for official HTTP evidence, effective-interval catalog rows and native `html_text` observations. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:23:| 21 | P1 | §§5.3, 5.6 | Adding `official_http` functions to `cnSpecOf` is insufficient: `cnSeriesSpecs()` selects only `collectableOf('akshare')`, and `cnSpecOf` itself rejects other adapters. `worldbank_csv` has no complete type/provider/evidence/ingestion contract. | Define adapter routing through export, collector, ingest schema, parser, storage and freshness. Add a full evidence-to-observation test per adapter. Keep unimplemented sources missing with `source:null`. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:24:| 22 | P1 | §§5.3, 5.5 | LPR observations are stored at month-end, then used to derive policy events. The event contract exposes `effective_date`, but quote dates are not preserved in its observation fields. This can move a rate event to month-end. Existing `PolicyEvent`, `IndicatorDetail` and `seriesExtras` are US-specific; CN cycles do not become available automatically. | Preserve official quote/effective dates explicitly; define a discriminated CN event shape and update all consumers and cycle dispatch. Test actual event-date boundaries and missing intervening months. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/02-spec-round1-codex.md:25:| 23 | P1 | §5.5 | Calling MLF carry-forward “不是插值” does not establish that an operation’s rate remains an effective policy rate until another operation. A missing announcement and a verified month without operations are indistinguishable; “完成见证” is undefined. The design can manufacture scored monthly values across collection gaps. | Require source-backed validity intervals and evidence of collection completeness, define the witness and `available_at`, and stop derivation across unverifiable gaps. Otherwise keep monthly MLF missing. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:1:| # | Severity P1/P2/P3 | Section | Defect | Concrete fix |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:3:| 1 | P1 | §2.4, §3.5 — C1 partially open | **The publication bypass is fixed, but the percentage-policy conflict remains.** P1 §3.2 says “没有 `percent` 字段就不显示任何百分比”; `display.ts` deliberately gives categorical cards `percent:null`. Draft 2 nevertheless renders `claim.distribution` as percentages while claiming compliance with P1. Reading stored regime data under the flag is permitted by P1’s server-read contract; that permission alone does **not** authorize displaying it without the eligible F4 card. | Explicitly amend P1 §3.2 to permit clearly labelled, non-probability rule shares **only behind the same-snapshot F4 publication gate**. Keep categorical `display.percent` null. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:4:| 2 | P1 | §3.4, §8 | **Failed reconciliation still publishes all numbers.** A duplicate contribution, wrong observation, or incorrect total produces `reconcile_failed`, but the spec requires normal numeric rendering plus a warning. That directly contradicts §8’s requirement that displayed contributions reconcile to the card. | Suppress the affected judgment numbers on invariants 1–6 failure. Return a number-free error to the normal UI; retain diagnostic differences in server logs or a separate diagnostic surface. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:5:| 3 | P1 | §3.3–§3.4 — C7 partially open | **The seven invariants do not validate the rule explanation.** Changing only `rule_id` leaves all seven checks passing: contributions, support, totals and F4 claim remain unchanged, while the UI displays another rule’s label. Stored support is also never checked against that rule’s authoritative coefficients. | Require the rule ID to exist under the row’s series type and stored support to equal its versioned rule support, treating omitted coefficients as zero. Do not reevaluate predicates. Add rule-ID/type/support corruption tests; the enumerated “seven” tests currently omit axis corruption. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:6:| 4 | P1 | §5.7–§5.8, §10 | **Registering v2 does not switch production to v2.** `judgments/compensate.ts` hard-codes `MACRO_METHOD.version='v1'` and searches the registry for it. Removing v1 makes compensation skip production. `strategy/playbooks.ts` and `strategy/deviations.ts` also accept only v1. The rollout’s “F4 produce … v2” is therefore unsupported. | Include producer selection, version-specific job markers, and downstream method acceptance in the migration. Test actual compensation after a v1→v2 registry change, then v2→v3—not just direct `gather→compute`. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:7:| 5 | P1 | §5.3, §5.8 — C21 still open | **`official_http` still cannot traverse ingestion.** The ingest route constructs evidence and retrieval records with `adapter:'akshare'`, then calls `ingestEvidence` with that adapter. `ingest.ts` rejects a series whose source adapter differs; its exhaustive `DATASET_OF:Record<Adapter,…>` also lacks `official_http`. Updating `cnSeriesSpecs` and parser dispatch does not fix this. | Derive the trusted adapter from the catalog, partition mixed-adapter retrievals before ingestion, preserve adapter identity on parse failures, and map `official_http` to the intended dataset. Add an end-to-end mixed AKShare/official-HTTP chunk test. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:8:| 6 | P1 | §4.4, §8 | **A fixed cutoff does not make preset inputs immutable.** `db.seriesHistory` filters `available_at` and current `status='ok'`; unlike `eligibleHistoryAsOf`, it does not enforce batch completion by cutoff. Evidence fetched before the snapshot but ingested/finalized afterward can enter a later request under the **same memo key**. Quarantine resolution can also change membership. Different hashes reveal the difference; they do not prevent it. | Freeze a durable input receipt, or define historical eligibility and an input-identity cache strategy that handles these transitions. Add a batch-finished-after-cutoff test and a quarantine-transition test. Do not claim identical hashes from `asOf` alone. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:10:| 8 | P1 | §3.3, §3.6, §8 | **Null-regime handling has mutually exclusive instructions.** §3.3 returns rows and coverage for non-classified results. §3.6 says an eligible F4 card with null regime is a mismatch, yet its presentation column still requires a decomposition table. D1 and the `f4_mismatch` rule forbid those numbers. The interface also declares `rows:ClockRow[]` while failure rules require `rows:null`. | Define a discriminated response union and one precedence order. Null regime plus eligible claim must be number-free `f4_mismatch`; genuine abstention must remain an explicit number-free state. Remove unreachable numeric states from acceptance fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:14:| 12 | P2 | §4.4 | **The preset route is not fully specified against the actual APIs.** `PRESETS[].form` omits the required UUID `request_id`, so it cannot directly pass `validateAnalysisRequest`. Preset `p6` is `event_window`: POST additionally reads both target-rate histories and derives events, contradicting “each group 2 reads.” The response cards also lack fields required by `RunJson`, including `kind`, `status`, and `created_at`. | Define request-ID handling, event inputs and read limits, and a dedicated presentation DTO or explicit `RunJson` adapter. Budget the two additional event-history reads. The explicit P1 amendment permits shared preset computation without stored runs; this is a bounded preset quota exception, not an arbitrary-analysis bypass. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:15:| 13 | P1 | §5.2, §5.7 | **Preserving `TRANSMISSION_V1` does not preserve replay by itself.** `buildTransmission` uses caller/default stages and returns current `VERSIONS.transmission`; it does not dispatch from the stored manifest’s method version. “`wiring` selects v2” does not define how old manifests retain v1 configuration and version labels. | Dispatch rendering and replay by the frozen manifest version, with immutable v1/v2 configurations. New manifest construction should explicitly choose v2. Test an existing v1 manifest after the default switches to v2. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/05-spec-round2-codex.md:20:Round-1 P1 dispositions verified against code:
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:47:| # | Severity P1/P2/P3 | Section | Defect | Concrete fix |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:49:| 1 | P1 | §3.2, §3.3, §5.2 | The proposed frozen `method_manifest` cannot produce the specified `ClockView`. It stores row id/type/weight/status/rule/support and denominator, but the DTO also requires current rule labels, unit, name, contribution values, comparison-role assignments, display change kind, observation metadata, and scoring/display membership. `clock-view.ts` must therefore continue importing current `regime.ts`/`catalog.ts`, making historical output dependent on live code. | Freeze a complete versioned manifest per result containing every projected row field: source/display metadata, unit, change kind, weight and denominator membership, observation IDs and comparison roles, rule id/label/support, contribution cells, and rule/weights/catalog versions. Make `clock-view.ts` consume only stored results plus this manifest; prohibit imports of `regime.ts`, `catalog.ts`, `WEIGHTS_V1`, and `RULES`; add a source/dependency assertion. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:50:| 2 | P1 | §3.3, §5.2 | The manifest still does not freeze the row-level score/support arithmetic inputs. `support` is nullable, while invariant 8 requires a default of zero; `rule_id` may be null but the invariant applies to it as though every row had a rule. The manifest schema does not distinguish “not matched” from “matched with missing support” or define the normalized support vector stored for every scoring row. | Define an exact schema, e.g. every scoring row has `rule_id:null` and `support:Record<Regime,number>` for nonmatches, or `rule_id:string` and the full four-state support vector for matches. State that missing support is normalized to zero before storage and reconciliation, and add schema/round-trip tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:51:| 3 | P1 | §3.2, §3.3 | The route reads observations before `db.regimeOf(id)`, while the priority table says an absent observation manifest is `integrity_error` and a missing result record is `record_missing`. There is no authoritative observation manifest source: `snapshot.observation_ids` may include display-only observations, while `method_manifest` contains rows but no explicit observation list or role mapping. The route can therefore report `integrity_error` for an irrelevant display observation while still lacking the data needed to validate each contribution’s observation ownership. | Store a complete observation manifest on the result or snapshot, including observation ID, series ID, period end, version, and comparison roles. Fetch only the observations required by the frozen result manifest. Define integrity failure as a missing/mismatched observation required by that manifest, and add tests distinguishing irrelevant observations from required observations. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:52:| 4 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4 | Invariant 1 says every contribution observation ID must be in the manifest, but the proposed method manifest schema contains no observation IDs. Invariant 8 likewise checks a stored `support` field that the schema makes nullable. The reconciliation contract is therefore not executable against the D1 storage format. | Include `observation_id` and `comparison_observation_ids/roles` in each scoring manifest row, or define a separate `observation_manifest`. Make rule/support fields non-null under explicit discriminated states. Update invariant 1 and 8 to reference the exact stored fields. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:53:| 5 | P1 | §2.4, §3.3, §5.7 | The F4 eligibility set `{open, settled, pending_fill}` includes `pending_fill`, but the supplied code’s canonical `Lifecycle` also includes it only as a type-level value; no code excerpt establishes how a pending-fill macro card is represented or whether it can carry a valid categorical claim. More importantly, the clock accepts `settled` and `pending_fill` as eligible, then requires invariant 7 to contain a claim, while an eligible pending card may not yet have a claim. | Define semantic eligibility separately from lifecycle: for example, require `open` or `settled` with a non-abstained categorical claim; define whether `pending_fill` is ever eligible and what claim it must contain. If pending cards are eligible without claims, exempt them from invariant 7 with a distinct nonnumeric state and test each lifecycle’s actual `toCard`/detail shape. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:54:| 6 | P1 | §2.4, §3.3, §3.4 | The spec retains v1’s `secondary_state !== null` requirement as an acknowledged `invalid_snapshot` abstention, but the clock’s invariant 7 and summary accept a null secondary. A one-state snapshot is therefore classified `f4_ineligible`, and the spec’s `f4_mismatch` example for “eligible card but snapshot regime null” does not resolve this case. | Specify whether v2 removes the secondary-state requirement. If v2 removes it, change the v2 factory and its tests accordingly and define how `margin_pp` treats a null secondary. If v1 semantics remain, define a dedicated `f4_invalid_secondary` state and ensure the single-state path cannot be mislabeled `f4_none`. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:55:| 7 | P1 | §3.2, §3.3 | The F4 lookup is underspecified. “List + detail” does not define how the clock selects among multiple eligible cards for the same snapshot, how duplicate judgments are resolved, or whether every matching card is fetched and reconciled. The route budget says two F4 HTTP requests, which cannot reconcile multiple same-snapshot cards. | Define deterministic card selection—e.g. require exactly one eligible same-snapshot card; if zero, `f4_none`; if more than one, fail closed with a dedicated status and deterministic IDs in diagnostics. Specify list filtering, detail fetch count, and tests for duplicates and multiple methods. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:56:| 8 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4 | The status precedence makes F4 eligibility precede storage reconciliation. A corrupt stored result with no eligible F4 card returns `f4_none` and never executes invariants 1–6/8, contradicting the requirement that reconciliation failures be detected and logged. The draft’s “1→6→8→7” order is only defined after F4 success. | Perform storage reconciliation independently of F4 availability. Use one precedence such as snapshot/version → storage record integrity/reconciliation → F4 availability/eligibility → F4 claim reconciliation. Ensure corrupt storage always yields `reconcile_failed`, even if F4 is absent, while preserving the required no-number behavior. Add cross-product tests for storage failure × each F4 state. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:57:| 9 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4 | `regime_status` is described as derived “in any state” from stored denominator, uncovered weight, and coverage threshold, but a storage-reconciliation failure is explicitly forbidden to expose numeric fields or numeric diagnostics. The formula `(denominator−uncovered)/denominator<0.6` therefore either leaks derived numeric state or cannot be computed reliably when reconciliation has failed. | Compute `regime_status` only from the frozen stored `regime`/coverage metadata before number exposure, or define a coarse stored status field. Do not recompute it from invalid reconciled numbers. Specify allowed metadata for `reconcile_failed` and test that no numeric fields or numeric diagnostics leak. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:58:| 10 | P1 | §3.4 | Invariant 4 requires `matched weight + uncovered_weight === denominator` “r6 exact,” but the code sums matched integer weights without rounding and stores only `uncoveredWeight`; no matched-weight field is stored in `macro_regime_results`. The invariant cannot be evaluated from the proposed storage. | Persist `matched_weight` and `denominator`, or define an exact derivation from the frozen manifest. State the arithmetic type and rounding, and add a persisted-result round-trip test. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:59:| 11 | P1 | §3.4 | Invariant 3 compares contribution sums with stored `scores`, but `scores` exist only inside a non-null `regime`; a stored null regime has contributions and uncovered weight but no score/coverage/axes/margin. The spec requires all invariants to run, yet a valid coverage-below-threshold result cannot satisfy invariants 3 and 5. | Define separate invariants for classified and null-regime records, or persist scores, denominator, matched weight, coverage, and axes independently of `regime`. Specify exactly which invariants apply when `regime=null` and test coverage-below-threshold and zero-score fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:60:| 12 | P1 | §3.4 | Invariant 6 says axes are recomputed from stored support/weight, but support is only present for matched rows and the manifest schema makes it nullable. Nonmatched rows contribute no axis numerator, so a nullable/missing support needs an explicit zero rule and complete type information. | Normalize every scoring row to a four-state support vector in the manifest, with zero support for nonmatches. State that only matched rows contribute to axis numerators and weights, and test rows with zero/partial support. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:61:| 13 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4 | The DTO’s `ClockRow.state` and `latest` contract conflicts with the frozen manifest: a `restricted` row can have no latest value, while `missing` and `no_data` are both defined but no exact assignment rule covers a manifest row with an observation that cannot be parsed. The proposed manifest has no canonical `latest` field. | Define the exact state decision table from frozen row fields: missing catalog definition, restricted definition, absent latest observation, invalid value, present but unmatched, matched. Freeze and project the selected latest observation explicitly; add one test per mutually exclusive row state. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:62:| 14 | P1 | §3.3 | The comparison-role algorithm remains ambiguous and does not match the existing classifier’s selected `prev`/`prev3m` inputs. “Latest comparison in the array is prev” can label an unrelated observation as `prev`, while quarterly `prev3m` is described as “previous quarter,” not the code’s exact selected comparison. The manifest schema does not freeze these roles. | Freeze the exact `latest`, `prev`, and `prev3m` observation IDs selected at publication time. Project roles directly from those IDs, permitting the same ID to have both roles. Do not infer `prev` from array position or dates in the view. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:63:| 15 | P1 | §5.2, §5.7 | D1 changes the snapshot’s observation union and catalog version but keeps `METHODOLOGY_VERSION='v1.0'`. The clock version gate compares `methodology_version` to the live constant and therefore accepts old snapshots under a changed catalog/method-manifest contract when their catalog hash happens to match current code. D1’s stated purpose does not create a distinct snapshot-selection semantics version. | Introduce a distinct frozen-selection version, such as `macro-regime-inputs/2`, stored on snapshots/results and checked by the clock. Do not reuse the classifier methodology version when selection, manifest, or denominator semantics change. Define old-snapshot rejection and version-transition tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:64:| 16 | P1 | §5.7 | The v2 hash plan is not deterministic: `deps` is said to be the same as v1, but v1’s `deps` list includes live `snapshots.ts`, `read-api.ts`, `freshness.ts`, and `db.ts`; the exact v2 module/import closure is unspecified, and later catalog changes can alter those dependencies. The “pin literal” only pins a catalog parameter, not the formula closure. | Specify the exact v2 `deps`, module path, imported symbol set, and closure hash inputs. Pin immutable method data and exclude live catalog implementation from the formula hash. Add a test proving an unrelated catalog edit leaves the formula hash unchanged while any formula/dependency edit changes it. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:65:| 17 | P1 | §5.7 | The production order says migration retires v1 before `sync-methodologies.ts` registers v2. During that interval, `compensate.ts` is changed to v2 but the database has no active/publishable v2 row, so compensation can skip with `methodology_missing`; existing v1 judgments may also stop resolving. | Register v2 successfully before switching producers and before retiring v1. Define rollback: migration should add v2 support without immediately retiring v1; deploy the code; sync/register v2; verify v2; switch consumers; only then retire v1 in a separate audited step. Add a deployment-state integration test. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:66:| 18 | P1 | §5.7 | The retirement migration updates `judgment_methodologies` and inserts an event but does not specify how `judgment_methodology_events.seq` is computed or how the event’s required fields are populated. The schema shown requires non-null seq/status/publishable/formula_hash/code_version/actor, while the spec only says “event row.” | Provide the complete idempotent SQL/RPC for retirement, including sequence allocation, copied `formula_hash`, `publishable`, `code_version`, actor, and duplicate-event handling. Test first run, rerun, and concurrent sync/retire behavior. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:67:| 19 | P1 | §5.3, §5.8 | The migration’s `official_http` CHECK update is incomplete for `macro_retrievals`. The migration comment says to handle it “if present,” but the ingest code writes retrievals with an adapter and the existing schema likely has the same legal set. Leaving its CHECK unchanged would reject official HTTP retrievals. | Include every adapter-bearing table in one normative migration, with exact table/column, old/new legal sets, discovered-constraint drop logic, and post-migration verification query. Do not make required ingest support conditional on an unverified table definition. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:68:| 20 | P1 | §5.3, §5.8 | The ingest route currently hardcodes `adapter:'akshare'` in both `EvidenceInput` and `putRetrieval`, and `DATASET_OF` is a two-key `Record<Adapter,...>`. Draft 3 says “infer adapter from catalog and group,” but gives no normative implementation boundary or validation that evidence body adapters agree with the inferred catalog adapter. Without this, an official-HTTP evidence object can be stored as AKShare and fail the retrieval CHECK. | Change the body contract to carry or derive a validated adapter per evidence item; compare it to `catalogById(series_id).source.adapter`; group by that adapter; pass the actual adapter to `ingestEvidence` and `putRetrieval`; extend `DATASET_OF` with `official_http`. Add mixed-adapter route and database-CHECK tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:69:| 21 | P1 | §5.3 | `official_http` is treated as both transport and parser/source kind, but the specification only mentions JSON pointer and HTML cell locators. It does not define how an adapter selects the parser, how content type maps to a parser, how unsupported MIME types fail, or whether the catalog’s source identifier is sufficient for `parserFor`. The current route selects only by `series_id`. | Define separate `transport_adapter='official_http'` and `parser_id/parser_version` fields. Specify allowed MIME types, JSON/HTML locator schemas, parser selection, unsupported-content behavior, and catalog/evidence consistency checks. Add JSON, HTML, wrong-MIME, and parser-failure fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:70:| 22 | P1 | §5.3 | The D1/D5 sequencing is contradictory. D1 adds `official_http` support and weekly infrastructure, while D2–D4 implement the China HTTP parsers; D5 adds the catalog rows. The spec does not define whether D1 is deployable without live official-HTTP catalog entries, or whether D5’s catalog addition requires another migration beyond the listed D1 migration. | State the activation invariant for each PR. D1 may add dormant infrastructure; D2–D4 remain inactive; D5 adds catalog/source rows and any required migration. Include catalog/version fixtures proving every deployed intermediate state is valid. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:71:| 23 | P1 | §4.4, §4.5 | The preset route claims to use `eligibleHistoryAsOf` “by cutoff completed batch,” but `eligibleHistoryAsOf` in the supplied code takes an `asOf` value and selects eligible observations; the spec does not define what “completed batch” means or how an in-progress/failed batch is excluded. The shared service contract is therefore not implementable from the shown DB API. | Specify the exact eligibility predicate and RPC invocation for `eligibleHistoryAsOf`, including batch status, observation availability/version selection, and treatment of failed/open batches. Use that exact API in both POST and presets and add a cutoff regression. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:72:| 24 | P1 | §4.4 | The shared service plan does not preserve POST’s existing semantics in one important respect: POST’s idempotency check occurs before reads and returns the persisted result for an existing request. The proposed `prepareAnalysis` description omits this route-level idempotency boundary. Moving POST to the service can accidentally perform reads/computation before discovering an existing run or change conflict behavior. | Define `prepareAnalysis` as pure preparation/computation only after POST’s request-id lookup and conflict check. Keep persistence/idempotency in POST and document the exact call sequence for both routes. Add existing-run, conflicting-spec, and read-failure tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:73:| 25 | P1 | §4.4 | The preset spec claims the same spec and cutoff produce the same `inputs_hash` and results as POST, but POST’s cutoff is request time while the preset cutoff is `snapshot.created_at`; these are different by definition. The test must compare the POST path invoked with an injectable cutoff equal to the preset cutoff, which the spec does not state. | Make cutoff an explicit POST test injection point and compare POST at `snapshot.created_at` with the preset. In production, retain POST’s now cutoff and clearly state that equality is only for the same supplied cutoff. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:74:| 26 | P1 | §4.4 | The memo key omits the correlation method behavior represented by `CORRELATION_VERSION`? It includes it, but it omits preset-specific specification/version and the canonicalization algorithm version. The same key is shared across all presets, so one preset’s frozen inputs can be returned for another. | Include `preset_id` and a `preset_spec_version` in the key, or key by the canonical preset spec hash. Include the code version that owns canonicalization and event derivation, and add cross-preset collision tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:75:| 27 | P1 | §4.4 | The response DTO includes `created_at:computed_at` while the shared memo may return a result computed earlier. This makes the field semantically false and prevents callers from distinguishing original computation time from response time. | Rename it to `computed_at` and preserve the true first-computation timestamp; if a response-generation timestamp is needed, add `served_at` separately. Test hot-cache responses retain the original timestamp. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:76:| 28 | P1 | §5.2 | The six-row field matrix remains non-normative because `firstPeriod` is `[D0a]`, yet D1 is said not to begin until the cells are backfilled. This is an unresolved prerequisite, not an implementation-ready contract. | Fill all six verified first-period values, source URLs, and retrieval dates before D1 starts. Make the table immutable except through a new draft/version; block the D1 implementation task on all cells being concrete. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:77:| 29 | P1 | §5.2 | The weekly release/freshness rule is internally inconsistent and does not match the actual publication convention stated in the table. WALCL is dated Wednesday and published Thursday, yet the invariant gives weekly next period as previous period + 7 days + lag + 3 days; with `releaseLagDays=1`, Friday becomes the expected next observation date. This can permanently mark normal weekly rows stale. | Define the expected observation period separately from its expected publication date. For a Wednesday-dated series published Thursday, the next period remains Wednesday+7 days while freshness compares against publication due time Thursday+lag/holiday grace. Test normal Thursday publication, delayed publication, and holidays. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:78:| 30 | P1 | §5.2 | The weekly table gives WALCL `releaseLagDays=1` but WTREGEN `1` as well, while WTREGEN’s value is a weekly average and its representative date is Wednesday. The source publication-day and availability semantics are not verified per series, so one generic weekly lag can be wrong. | Put the verified publication-day convention and `available_at` basis in each series definition; distinguish representative observation date from publication date; test each series independently. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:79:| 31 | P1 | §5.2 | The WTREGEN monthly derivation is not numerically defined. `month_end` selects the last observation in the calendar month and labels the derived row at month end, but the source is a weekly average; the spec says “last weekly observation” without defining whether a month’s partial final week is included or how its average period is identified. | Define a dedicated aggregation rule with canonical week intervals, inclusion of partial weeks, and whether month-end rows use the last complete week, last observation, or an official monthly average. Store source interval IDs and prohibit treating a partial week as a complete weekly average. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:80:| 32 | P1 | §5.3 | The LPR monthly rule says `month_end` from quote observations, but the existing `derive.ts` `month_end` implementation skips the newest month until a later-month witness arrives and skips any month whose input month is the newest observed month. The draft acknowledges a delay but still calls the monthly series “that month’s last quote,” which can mislead implementers and users. | Define the exact LPR derivation semantics as a distinct rule or rename the concept to “completed month with later-month witness.” Specify which quote is selected, the witness, `available_at`, and cutoff behavior; do not rely on generic `month_end` without defining its exception. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:81:| 33 | P1 | §5.3 | The LPR parser’s row-key policy is incomplete for repeated quotes: it rejects conflicting same-day rows but says identical duplicates only increment a count. It does not define whether the quote date, tenor, and value must all match, nor how a same-day multi-tenor page is separated from duplicate rows. | Define the full natural key `(quote_date, tenor, product_type)` and define duplicate versus conflict. Add fixtures for same-day 1Y/5Y rows, identical duplicates, and conflicting duplicates. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:82:| 34 | P1 | §5.3 | The LPR event derivation is not specified against the data contract. It says `effective_date=quote date` and `policy_rate_change`, but does not define the previous comparable quote, missing-history behavior, first observation, tenor selection, or whether unchanged quotes generate events. | Define event identity, previous-quote lookup, first-observation behavior, unchanged-quote policy, and tenor scope. Add event fixtures and specify that events never silently bridge missing quote periods. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:83:| 35 | P1 | §5.4 | The AFRE revision policy remains underdefined for a revisable series. “Later page publication wins” is not enough when pages overlap, have equal publication dates, are later corrected, or contain different values for the same period. The spec also says the same-page duplicate is rejected but does not define cross-page conflicts. | Define canonical row key, page publication timestamp/version, equal-timestamp tie handling, cross-page conflict behavior, and whether a later revision supersedes or quarantines the prior value. Add multi-page, equal-timestamp, and reissue fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:84:| 36 | P1 | §5.5 | MLF monthly aggregation remains ambiguous. “That month’s final 1Y operation” may select by date, but the monthly row’s value, period label, and completion witness are not defined, and the code’s generic `month_end` requires a later-month operation. | Specify operation identity, monthly selection by latest valid operation date, value source, completion witness, `available_at`, and behavior for no-operation months. Add same-month multi-operation and next-month-witness fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:85:| 37 | P1 | §5.2, §5.8 | D1’s catalog changes include weekly rows, but the migration only alters `native_frequency`; it does not add or validate `firstPeriod`, `releaseLagDays`, and other new catalog fields or explain how the six rows are persisted in `macro_series`. The spec references a new `MacroSeriesDef.firstPeriod?`, but the database schema shown has no such column and the migration does not add it. | Add the required database column and migration, backfill/verification strategy, and catalog loader mapping. Ensure the persisted six-row definitions match the normative field matrix and add migration/load round-trip tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:86:| 38 | P1 | §5.2 | `method_manifest` is written through `publishSnapshot`/`publishRegion`, but the publication path is a C6-sensitive file and the spec does not define whether the manifest is built from the exact input set used by `classifyRegime` or from a separately rebuilt catalog list. Separate reconstruction can omit rows or observations. | Build the manifest inside the same publication transaction from the exact resolved scoring/display inputs passed to `classifyRegime`. Persist one canonical manifest and have the result and snapshot reference or contain it. Add a publication fixture proving manifest rows exactly match classifier input and denominator. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:87:| 39 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4 | Invariant 5 compares `pickStates(norm)` and `margin_pp` to stored values but does not define the exact input `norm` used for recomputation. The code’s `pickStates` uses unrounded norms for primary/secondary and stores rounded norms; recomputing from stored rounded norms can change ties or ordering. | Specify the publication algorithm precisely: recompute from the same unrounded score totals and operation order, then round outputs. Freeze enough score/axis intermediates or store an explicit selection trace. Add tie-boundary and half-ULP tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:1:## Round-1 disposition
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:52:## New defects in draft 2
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:54:| # | Severity P1/P2/P3 | Section | Defect | Concrete fix |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:56:| 1 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4, §3.6 | The route-to-status order is contradictory. The route says “version gate → `regimeOf` → F4,” while §3.3 says F4 failure sets `regime/quadrants/rows/coverage/reconciliation/axes` to null. Consequently `record_missing`, `coverage_below_threshold`, and `zero_score` can never be returned when no F4 card exists, despite §3.6 claiming they are card-level states. This collapses distinct missing-data causes into F4 failure. | Define a single precedence order. Recommended: snapshot/version → `regimeOf` presence and stored regime status → F4 availability/eligibility → claim reconciliation. Return storage failure states before consulting F4, while exposing no regime numbers for null/coverage/zero-score records. Add tests for every ordering combination. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:57:| 2 | P1 | §2.5 C6, §3.1, §3.3 | The server-side `clock-view.ts` must validate stored contributions, weights, support, rules, and F4 claims. The draft only prohibits client imports of `regime.ts`/`catalog.ts` and says the projection is a pure function. It does not prohibit server-side `clock-view.ts` from importing `regime.ts`, which would pull current `WEIGHTS_V1`, current catalog status, and current `RULES` into historical snapshot reconciliation. That is a second method interpretation and defeats the claimed snapshot freeze. | Freeze a per-result method manifest containing row id, type, weight, matched rule id, support, contribution, and denominator membership. `clock-view.ts` must consume only that manifest and stored results; it must not import `regime.ts`, `catalog.ts`, `WEIGHTS_V1`, or `RULES`. Add a dependency/source assertion for both server and client bundles. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:58:| 3 | P1 | §2.4, §3.3, §3.8 | F4’s detail parser requires every `judgment.inputs` entry to contain a nested `snapshot` object (`nonemptyObj(x.snapshot)`), while the clock only needs an input reference with `kind:'macro_snapshot'` and `ref.snapshot_id`. A legitimate F4 card can therefore be classified as malformed solely because the detail payload lacks the parser’s extra field. The new `f4-card.ts` is supposed to rewrite the contract, but the spec still refers to reusing `f4-client.ts`, whose semantics are narrower and number-free. | Specify a clock-specific F4 schema validator that requires only the fields used by the clock: judgment id, methodology id/version, lifecycle, calibration, display, claim, and a `macro_snapshot` input with `snapshot_id`. Reject unknown shapes as malformed, but do not inherit the unrelated `x.snapshot` requirement. Contract-test against the actual `/api/judgments/:id` payload. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:59:| 4 | P1 | §2.4, §3.3 | The F4 lifecycle set `{open, settled, pending_fill}` does not match the code’s `JudgmentCard` lifecycle vocabulary. The code can emit `abstained`, `void`, `withdrawn`, and `unknown`; `pending_fill` is not defined as a lifecycle value. The matching rule therefore risks treating every real pending card as ineligible or using a nonexistent state. | Enumerate the canonical F4 lifecycle enum from the shared judgment types and define eligibility as an explicit subset of that enum. Define what “pending” actually is. If `pending_fill` is a separate field, model it separately rather than inventing a lifecycle. Test every lifecycle using actual `toCard` output. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:60:| 5 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4 | The F4 eligibility rule and reconciliation rule conflict with the existing v1 implementation. `macro-regime.v1.ts` requires `secondary_state !== null` and aborts with `invalid_snapshot` when there is no secondary state, but the clock accepts any `regime` and computes `secondary ?? 0`. A valid one-state result can exist in the macro engine yet can never produce an F4 card. The clock’s `f4_none` then hides that fact. | State explicitly whether v2 changes the F4 requirement that a secondary state exists. If it remains required, the clock must expose a distinct `f4_invalid_secondary` status. If it is removed, implement it in the new v2 method and test single-state cases. Do not silently conflate this with “no card.” |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:61:| 6 | P1 | §5.2, §5.7 | D0 changes `catalogItemsOf` to all indicators and makes `displayInputsOf` include all active monthly/quarterly weight-zero rows, but the specification says the clock’s `non_scoring` list is “manifest 中不在 WEIGHTS_V1 的指标.” Existing active indicator rows that are in `WEIGHTS_V1` but excluded by `displayInputsOf`—or all scoring indicators generally—are not a coherent definition of “non-scoring.” The projection can also omit a display-only observation or label a scoring row non-scoring. | Define `scoring_rows` explicitly as the frozen `regionWeights` manifest and `display_only_rows` as the frozen display manifest minus scoring rows. Return each manifest entry with its source, role, and scoring membership. Do not infer membership from the current `WEIGHTS_V1` object. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:62:| 7 | P1 | §5.2, §5.7 | D0 explicitly changes `snapshots.ts`, but the “v2 factory” still has no stated `deps` list. The current v1 `deps` include `snapshots.ts`, `read-api.ts`, `flags.ts`, `freshness.ts`, and `db.ts`; if v2 omits or changes them, formula-hash behavior differs materially. If it imports them, the planned v2 hash still becomes invalid on every later catalog change. The spec therefore has not made the v1→v2 drift problem deterministic. | Specify the exact v2 `deps`, `module`, and import closure, and state which files are intentionally outside the formula hash. If catalog changes must not drift the method, pin the catalog parameter separately and exclude the live catalog implementation from the hash. Add a test that changes an unrelated catalog row in a fixture and asserts the v2 formula hash remains stable while a parameter change creates a new version. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:63:| 8 | P1 | §5.7 | Removing v1 from `REGISTRY` does not retire its stored row, and the only retirement plan is an owner action after checking unspecified script behavior. The production order also omits any v1 retirement or migration step. This leaves an active method row whose runtime dependencies may no longer match the deployed registry. | Make retirement deterministic in the same release: add and test a `retired` transition, update the registry/sync plan, and preserve the stored v1 row and audit event. If retirement cannot be automated, block production rollout on an explicit verified SQL/RPC procedure and include it in §5.7 and the runbook. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:64:| 9 | P1 | §5.3, §5.8 | The D2/D3/D4 rows are described as “目录不改,” but the proposed active scoring rows already exist as `missing`, and D2/D3/D4 add event-source rows, source objects, parser identifiers, event versions, and possibly derived rows. Any catalog row or source change changes `CATALOG_VERSION`; the PR matrix’s “no method version” statement is therefore inconsistent with C1. | Separate catalog definitions from collector/parser code. State exactly which files may be added without changing the catalog, and which row/source changes force a new catalog hash and F4 method version. D5 may flip only pre-existing stable missing rows if that does not alter their serialized definitions. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:65:| 10 | P1 | §4.4, §8 | The preset route claims “same `validateAnalysisRequest` → `freezeInputs` → `runAnalysis`” as POST, but POST also checks a verifiable `code_version` before reading, uses a UTC request-time cutoff, resolves aliases, handles `event_window` target rows, limits monthly/daily history lengths, and persists failures through the quota RPC. The preset path omits or changes these semantics. “Same result” is not therefore defined. | Extract one shared `prepareAnalysis` service that owns validation, code-version resolution, cutoff selection, alias resolution, history limits, event derivation, input freezing, and analysis execution. Both POST and presets must call it. The preset may substitute the snapshot cutoff for “now,” but that substitution must be an explicit parameter and must be reflected in `inputs_hash`. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:66:| 11 | P1 | §4.4 | The preset specification does not address the POST route’s quota RPC semantics, so it does not prove that presets are outside quota. Quota may be enforced in middleware or another shared helper even if `create_macro_analysis_run` is not called. | Name the exact quota-enforcement points and add an integration test proving that invoking a preset does not increment any user, plan, IP, or global quota counter. Also test concurrency and memo behavior under quota exhaustion. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:67:| 12 | P1 | §4.4, §8 | Preset memoization is described as process-local, but “同一快照下不同实例、不同时间得到相同冻结输入” is not guaranteed. `seriesHistory(asOf=cutoff)` filters by availability time, while `freezeInputs` can include revisions or additional eligible history selected at read time. A later deployment or collector run can change the rows returned without changing `snapshot_id`, `CATALOG_VERSION`, or `code_version`. The memo key therefore does not cover all frozen inputs. | Include the canonical `inputs_hash` in the persisted/result cache identity, or persist the frozen preset inputs under a versioned immutable key. Do not claim cross-instance determinism unless the historical read is pinned by an immutable data revision or persisted frozen-input record. Add a regression with a later revision at the same cutoff. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:68:| 13 | P1 | §4.4 | The preset route’s `code_version` is not defined. The existing POST fails with `code_version_unavailable` when no verifiable commit exists. The draft names `code_version` in the memo key and response but gives no resolver or failure rule, so a non-verifiable deployment could produce non-replayable presets or silently use an unstable version. | Require `resolveCodeVersion()` with the same fail-closed rule as POST. Return `code_version_unavailable` before reading data when no verifiable version exists; never substitute a local timestamp or mutable source hash. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:69:| 14 | P1 | §5.2, §5.8 | The migration constraint handling is not deployable as written. The SQL excerpt shows unnamed table-level frequency and adapter checks. The draft says to “drop constraint” by name and later says to verify the actual name, but `ALTER TABLE ... DROP CONSTRAINT` cannot be implemented until the exact name is known. “Constraint names are verified later” is not an executable migration plan. | Supply the exact current constraint names from the deployed schema, or provide `DO $$ ... information_schema ... $$` logic that drops the discovered check safely. Include the exact `ADD CONSTRAINT` names, old and new legal sets, and SQL validation queries in the spec and migration. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:70:| 15 | P1 | §5.2, §5.8, §5.9 | D1’s weekly catalog rows are given `releaseLagDays:2`, but §5.8 says the existing exact-list test requires daily/event/month_end rows to have `lag undefined` and asks to treat weekly like monthly “允许 lag.” The actual assertion is not shown, and this exemption changes an existing catalog invariant. The proposed weekly release rule also needs to handle holidays and the FRED publication-day convention. | State the exact new invariant for each native frequency. Provide the revised assertion rather than a prose exception. Define the next expected observation date from an explicit calendar/holiday rule and test Thursday, Friday, holiday, and delayed-publication cases. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:71:| 16 | P1 | §5.2 | The WTREGEN description and monthly derivation disagree. The table calls WTREGEN a “week average” with Wednesday `period_end`, while the derived monthly row is “month内最后一次周平均观测.” A weekly average labeled with a Wednesday date is not necessarily the average for that Wednesday’s week, and `month_end` selects the final observation, not necessarily the week covering month-end. | Define the exact source period represented by each weekly observation and the exact monthly selection rule. If the source is a week average, store a canonical week identifier/interval or define the representative date. Do not label a point observation as a weekly average without source evidence. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:72:| 17 | P1 | §5.3 | The LPR monthly derivation uses the existing `month_end` implementation, whose comments and behavior require a later-month “completion witness” and do not derive the newest observed month. Therefore the current month’s last LPR quote will not produce the monthly row even though the draft says “该月最后一次报价.” The rule also delays availability until a quote in the following month is observed. | Either document and accept this exact behavior, including the following-month witness and cutoff impact, or define a separate quote-month derivation rule. Do not describe the existing `month_end` output as an immediately available last quote in the current month. Add tests for month-end quote, following-month quote, and delayed publication. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:73:| 18 | P1 | §5.3–§5.5 | `official_http` is added to `Adapter`, but `SeriesSource.adapter` and `EvidenceInput.adapter` then require official HTTP series to use `official_http`. The existing generic evidence/ingest paths shown assume CSV/JSON parsing and CSV evidence conventions. The draft does not define whether HTML bytes and JSON bytes share one adapter, how the locator maps to a JSON pointer, or how the MIME/evidence CHECK is validated. | Define `official_http` as a transport adapter and add a separate parser/source kind for JSON and HTML. Specify the allowed content types, locator mapping, MIME validation, evidence object naming, and error behavior for unsupported responses. Keep adapter semantics consistent across `SeriesSource`, `EvidenceInput`, and the database CHECK. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:74:| 19 | P1 | §5.4 | The PBOC AFRE collector is specified to “backfill” by traversing list links and to treat a same-month reissue as a new version, but the row-key/duplicate policy in §0.1 covers only the LPR parser. The AFRE parser lacks a canonical revision-selection rule and a deterministic rule for selecting among multiple monthly pages. | Define the AFRE row key, effective-period key, revision ordering, and “latest authoritative revision” rule. Preserve every revision with evidence, select the highest valid revision deterministically, and reject conflicting pages. Add official-bytes fixtures for reissue and multiple-page cases. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:75:| 20 | P1 | §5.5 | The MLF plan assumes a PBOC table with date, amount, and winning rate, but the monthly `CN_MLF_1Y` definition does not state which table column supplies the rate or how multiple operations in one month are selected. “最后一次操作的中标利率” is not a sufficient data contract without operation identity and revision semantics. | Specify the exact source column and operation identifier, define duplicate and multi-operation selection, and make the monthly row a deterministic operation-level selection. Include the operation date and source locator in evidence. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:76:| 21 | P1 | §4.4, §8 | The acceptance criterion “same spec and cutoff ⇒ same `inputs_hash` and results” is not implementable from the existing request contract. `validateAnalysisRequest` and the POST body include a required `request_id`; a preset spec cannot be byte-identical to a user POST without inventing a request id and potentially violating canonical-spec equality. The input hash may also include request metadata rather than only frozen observations. | Define canonical preset specs without user request metadata and a separate canonical analysis body for comparison. Specify exactly which fields are included in `inputs_hash`; do not include `request_id`, `computed_at`, or other response-only fields. Test the shared canonicalization function. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/06-spec-round2-mmx.md:77:| 22 | P1 | §4.4, §8 | The fixed eight preset specs are referenced as `PRESETS`, but no authoritative definition, IDs, region mapping, request payloads, or “same spec as POST” fixtures are supplied in the spec. This leaves a central acceptance target unimplementable and unprovable. | Add a normative preset table or code fixture containing all eight `preset_id`, region, canonical spec, expected cutoff, expected input hash, and expected success/error. Use the same fixtures in route, POST, and browser acceptance tests. |
