docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:54:T-M54: still open (the AFRE table identifier and exact row/column/unit contract remain unspecified)
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:68:| 65 | P1 | §3.4 | Invariant 5 still recomputes `N'` from rounded r6 contribution cells. The publication code accumulates unrounded score cells and rounds only when storing `scores` and `norm`; summing rounded contributions can change the denominator or tie ordering, so the stated reconstruction is not the publication algorithm. | Persist the unrounded score totals used for normalization, or define and persist a canonical selection trace. Reconcile against those exact totals, with stored `scores` and `norm` checked as separately rounded projections; add accumulation and tie-boundary fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:72:| 69 | P1 | §5.7 | The production sequence still creates an invalid method window: migration retires v1, then `sync-methodologies.ts` is expected to register v2, but consumers are switched to v2 in the same deployment. A sync failure leaves no active/publishable macro method; before sync, old code’s v1 output is deliberately rejected. | Split rollout into: deploy v2-compatible code without switching producers; sync/register and verify publishable v2; switch producers/consumers; publish and verify an A snapshot; only then retire v1 in a separate migration/job. Add failure/rollback tests for each stage. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:73:| 70 | P1 | §5.7, §5.8 | The retirement operation is not an idempotent transactional contract. The SQL sketch derives `seq` via `max(seq)+1`, which races under concurrent execution, and the event’s `reason`, `occurred_at`, exact `publishable`, `formula_hash`, and `code_version` source are not fixed. Re-running may create duplicate status-change events or violate audit expectations. | Use a locked/serializable transaction or an atomic RPC that locks the methodology row, allocates the next sequence, updates status, and inserts exactly one event with all required fields copied from the methodology row. Define rerun behavior and unique-event semantics; test first run, rerun, and concurrent execution. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:74:| 71 | P1 | §5.3, §5.8 | `official_http` is added to the evidence-object CHECK, but the supplied ingest path writes retrievals through `putRetrieval`, and the draft provides no normative schema/migration verification for every retrieval adapter-bearing column. If `macro_retrievals.adapter` has a restrictive CHECK, official HTTP ingestion remains undeployable. | Add the exact `macro_retrievals.adapter` column and CHECK to the normative migration inventory; migrate it to allow `official_http` if present, or state and verify that it has no adapter CHECK. Include insertion tests for both evidence objects and retrievals. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:77:| 74 | P1 | §5.3, §5.4, §5.5 | The draft authorizes parsers and catalog rows for official HTTP sources, but does not define how the parsed numeric values are bound to the catalog series, period, unit, and policy rate event semantics. The existing ingest path relies on claims and `claimPeriodEnd`, while the parser contracts only specify some row keys; a successful parse could therefore produce values with an incorrect unit or period mapping. | For each new series/parser, pin the canonical value-to-series mapping, output period label, numeric unit, decimal policy, and event-source type. Add parser-to-ingest integration tests asserting the written `series_id`, `period_end`, `value_text`, and unit. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:78:| 75 | P1 | §5.2, §5.8 | D1’s catalog contract requires `firstPeriod`, release-lag semantics, and six new rows, but `macro_series` has no `first_period` column and the migration only changes `native_frequency`. The catalog loader’s persistence mapping for `firstPeriod` and the six rows is therefore undefined, so D1 cannot persist its own normative data. | Decide whether `firstPeriod` is code-only or persisted. If persisted, add the exact column, backfill/verification procedure, loader mapping, and round-trip tests. If code-only, state that the catalog loader validates it against source metadata and provide the six concrete values before D1 begins. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/12-spec-round4-mmx.md:89:| 86 | P2 | §5.8 | The constraint-discovery regex is anchored to one exact PostgreSQL rendering (`^CHECK \(\(adapter = ANY`) and performs no verification after replacing the constraint. A formatting difference or additional restrictive constraint can leave the intended state unenforced or falsely pass migration tests. | Discover constraints by `pg_get_constraintdef` structure with a tolerant parser, list and verify all affected constraints in a migration precondition, and after replacement query `pg_constraint` to assert the new constraint and absence of obsolete adapter/frequency checks. Add tests using equivalent constraint spellings and an extra restrictive constraint. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:49:| 1 | P1 | §3.2, §3.3, §5.2 | The proposed frozen `method_manifest` cannot produce the specified `ClockView`. It stores row id/type/weight/status/rule/support and denominator, but the DTO also requires current rule labels, unit, name, contribution values, comparison-role assignments, display change kind, observation metadata, and scoring/display membership. `clock-view.ts` must therefore continue importing current `regime.ts`/`catalog.ts`, making historical output dependent on live code. | Freeze a complete versioned manifest per result containing every projected row field: source/display metadata, unit, change kind, weight and denominator membership, observation IDs and comparison roles, rule id/label/support, contribution cells, and rule/weights/catalog versions. Make `clock-view.ts` consume only stored results plus this manifest; prohibit imports of `regime.ts`, `catalog.ts`, `WEIGHTS_V1`, and `RULES`; add a source/dependency assertion. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:65:| 17 | P1 | §5.7 | The production order says migration retires v1 before `sync-methodologies.ts` registers v2. During that interval, `compensate.ts` is changed to v2 but the database has no active/publishable v2 row, so compensation can skip with `methodology_missing`; existing v1 judgments may also stop resolving. | Register v2 successfully before switching producers and before retiring v1. Define rollback: migration should add v2 support without immediately retiring v1; deploy the code; sync/register v2; verify v2; switch consumers; only then retire v1 in a separate audited step. Add a deployment-state integration test. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:66:| 18 | P1 | §5.7 | The retirement migration updates `judgment_methodologies` and inserts an event but does not specify how `judgment_methodology_events.seq` is computed or how the event’s required fields are populated. The schema shown requires non-null seq/status/publishable/formula_hash/code_version/actor, while the spec only says “event row.” | Provide the complete idempotent SQL/RPC for retirement, including sequence allocation, copied `formula_hash`, `publishable`, `code_version`, actor, and duplicate-event handling. Test first run, rerun, and concurrent sync/retire behavior. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:67:| 19 | P1 | §5.3, §5.8 | The migration’s `official_http` CHECK update is incomplete for `macro_retrievals`. The migration comment says to handle it “if present,” but the ingest code writes retrievals with an adapter and the existing schema likely has the same legal set. Leaving its CHECK unchanged would reject official HTTP retrievals. | Include every adapter-bearing table in one normative migration, with exact table/column, old/new legal sets, discovered-constraint drop logic, and post-migration verification query. Do not make required ingest support conditional on an unverified table definition. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:70:| 22 | P1 | §5.3 | The D1/D5 sequencing is contradictory. D1 adds `official_http` support and weekly infrastructure, while D2–D4 implement the China HTTP parsers; D5 adds the catalog rows. The spec does not define whether D1 is deployable without live official-HTTP catalog entries, or whether D5’s catalog addition requires another migration beyond the listed D1 migration. | State the activation invariant for each PR. D1 may add dormant infrastructure; D2–D4 remain inactive; D5 adds catalog/source rows and any required migration. Include catalog/version fixtures proving every deployed intermediate state is valid. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:83:| 35 | P1 | §5.4 | The AFRE revision policy remains underdefined for a revisable series. “Later page publication wins” is not enough when pages overlap, have equal publication dates, are later corrected, or contain different values for the same period. The spec also says the same-page duplicate is rejected but does not define cross-page conflicts. | Define canonical row key, page publication timestamp/version, equal-timestamp tie handling, cross-page conflict behavior, and whether a later revision supersedes or quarantines the prior value. Add multi-page, equal-timestamp, and reissue fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:85:| 37 | P1 | §5.2, §5.8 | D1’s catalog changes include weekly rows, but the migration only alters `native_frequency`; it does not add or validate `firstPeriod`, `releaseLagDays`, and other new catalog fields or explain how the six rows are persisted in `macro_series`. The spec references a new `MacroSeriesDef.firstPeriod?`, but the database schema shown has no such column and the migration does not add it. | Add the required database column and migration, backfill/verification strategy, and catalog loader mapping. Ensure the persisted six-row definitions match the normative field matrix and add migration/load round-trip tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:87:| 39 | P1 | §3.3, §3.4 | Invariant 5 compares `pickStates(norm)` and `margin_pp` to stored values but does not define the exact input `norm` used for recomputation. The code’s `pickStates` uses unrounded norms for primary/secondary and stores rounded norms; recomputing from stored rounded norms can change ties or ordering. | Specify the publication algorithm precisely: recompute from the same unrounded score totals and operation order, then round outputs. Freeze enough score/axis intermediates or store an explicit selection trace. Add tie-boundary and half-ULP tests. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:88:| 40 | P2 | §3.3, §3.4 | `ClockMeta.reconciliation.tolerance` is typed as a number only in the `ok` branch, while the invariant tolerance depends on the matched-row count. The DTO does not define the tolerance in failure diagnostics, although diagnostics are forbidden from containing numeric fields. | Either make the tolerance a documented non-displayed DTO constant or omit it. If retained, define that it is a unit constant, not a measured result, and keep failed diagnostics numeric-free. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:90:| 42 | P2 | §3.3 | The priority table says `f4_earlier` selects the newest `as_of` card when cards exist but point to another snapshot, but there may be several cards for different earlier snapshots with the same `as_of`; no tie-breaker is defined. | Define a deterministic tie-breaker, such as newest registered/created timestamp then judgment ID, and return conflicting IDs in diagnostics. Add equal-`as_of` fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:102:| 54 | P2 | §5.4 | The AFRE “official YoY column only” requirement does not define the exact table title, row label, unit, or percentage representation, so parsers can silently accept a different percentage column. | Pin the official table identifier, exact row/column labels, unit, and accepted numeric format in the normative parser contract and fixtures. |
docs/audits/2026-11-07-macro-p5/09-spec-round3-mmx.md:109:| 61 | P2 | §5.8 | The migration’s dynamic constraint lookup selects only one constraint name into a scalar variable and drops at most one. A table may have multiple checks involving `native_frequency` or `adapter`; leaving another restrictive check would make inserts fail. | Use a set-returning query to discover every matching CHECK, drop all of them
